I’ll inspect the metadata, diffraction pattern, and the reusable analysis/refinement utilities first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine them in GSAS-II with staged profile/structure checks, and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project all agree.

The chemistry points first toward lead tungstate formation, but the unknown phase count means I’ll treat residual WO₃/Pb oxides and alternative Pb–W–O compounds as live hypotheses. I’m now extracting peak families and checking which crystallographic databases and GSAS-II interfaces are available locally.

Peak-family matching now supports three phases: broad scheelite PbWO₄, sharper monoclinic WO₃, and residual tetragonal Pb₃O₄. The shared offset is nearly constant in 2θ (about +0.4°), which argues for a common angular zero correction rather than implausibly shrinking every cell. I’m setting up staged GSAS-II trials, including alternate WO₃ and PbWO₄ records and a check for PbO.

The staged profile tests are discriminating cleanly: size broadening fits PbWO₄ and Pb₃O₄ better than strain-only models (about 46 nm and 33 nm apparent coherent-domain sizes). I’m now transferring the narrow WO₃ width into the common instrument profile where appropriate, because a phase-size term and the common Lorentzian \(X/\cos\theta\) term can otherwise be nearly indistinguishable.

The physically screened shortlist is ready. I’m now comparing the viable WO₃ and PbWO₄ records under the same staged refinement schedule, including explicit tests for raspite/defect scheelite and residual PbO, so the phase call does not depend on profile compensation.

The control confirms why fit alone is insufficient: the crude monoclinic WO₃ record reaches \(R_\mathrm{wp}\approx6.88\%\), but its 1.58 Å O–O contact is chemically untenable. The physical orthorhombic model is already distinctly worse; the higher-quality triclinic model is still converging.

The physical triclinic WO₃ record fits better than orthorhombic but still leaves a clear penalty (\(R_\mathrm{wp}\approx7.75\%\)) and drives asymmetry to its lower bound, so I am not accepting it. I’m expanding the exact-WO₃ inventory for a physically valid monoclinic record before deciding whether the tungsten oxide polymorph is identifiable.

A physically sound neutron monoclinic WO₃ model now improves the fit to \(R_\mathrm{wp}=6.74\%\), outperforming both the invalid control and the other valid polymorphs with stable, interior profile terms. Residual PbO collapses to zero scale, while the defect scheelite model is worse and introduces a 97% zero/asymmetry correlation.

The phase inventory is now settled at three phases. I’m doing the final profile challenge on the accepted physical model—common Lorentzian width versus WO₃-specific size or strain—then I’ll replay the pruned model from disk and audit residual peaks, correlations, geometry, and crystalline mass balance before export.

The replay converged cleanly (maximum shift/esd 0.014) with no frozen variables. The formal crystalline fractions are about 62.1% PbWO₄, 21.8% WO₃, and 16.1% Pb₃O₄; I’m extending the final replay to the few edge points outside 10–100° so the submitted profile covers every measured point, then exporting and validating the CIFs.

The CIF audit passes: all three exports reproduce the intended compositions and symmetries, with shortest bonds/contacts physically sensible (PbWO₄ W–O 1.777 Å; WO₃ W–O 1.716 Å and O–O 2.539 Å; Pb₃O₄ Pb–O 2.131 Å). A fixed-ADP sensitivity check changes fractions by under 0.2 wt%, but indicates the arbitrary default \(U_\mathrm{iso}=0.01\) over-damps high-angle intensity; I’m testing one constrained common ADP before freezing the final intensity model.

The final model is now frozen and documented. Bound-seeking polarization, roughness, and common-ADP alternatives were excluded even though they lower the residual; their fraction spread is about 1 wt%, which is recorded as the practical model uncertainty rather than hiding it behind the smaller formal esds. I’m running the final cross-file consistency checks now.

Completed the GSAS-II refinement and validated all outputs.

Identified crystalline phases:

- PbWO₄ scheelite: 62.05 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 21.83 wt%
- Pb₃O₄ minium: 16.12 wt%

Final fit: \(R_\mathrm{wp}=6.732\%\), \(R_\mathrm{p}=4.579\%\), GOF = 2.714. Fractions are normalized crystalline values; practical model uncertainty is approximately 1 wt% absolute, and amorphous content is not quantified.

